  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nConvection is waning tonight over the Middle Texas waters, but \nexpect additional development over the next couple of days in that \narea as deep moisture sits over the western Gulf. Any rain chance \nfor South Texas itself are minimal and mainly confined to the \nimmediate coast. \n\nAs we head toward the middle of the week things get a bit \ncomplicated again in terms of rain chances. Current trends suggest \nmoisture pushes back inland by Wednesday into Thursday and would \ngive us at least some morning streamer activity along the coast and \na return of sea breeze convection.  However, will have to watch the \nmid-level disturbance in the Bay of Campeche as a surface low could \ndevelop on that as conditions improve mid to late week. If this does \ndevelop, it would rob a lot of the moisture from our area, so timing \nis going to be key to see if we get convection in here Wednesday \nbefore any development occurs.  While there is plenty of discrepancy \nin guidance as to what would happen with a system, the general \nconsensus is for a northerly track, staying east of the Texas coast. \nThis would also wrap drier air around into the western Gulf, thus \ndecreasing rain chances as previously mentioned.  If no development \noccurs though, would expect higher rain chances late in the week. \n\nDecreasing deep moisture and increasing mid-level temperatures will \nlead to a gradual increase in temperatures through the first part of \nthe work week. We start out today with highs mainly in the 80s, \nand expect highs in the lower to mid 90s for most of the area by \nthe middle and latter part of the week. Low temperatures will be \nin the upper 60s and lower 70s. \n\nNortheast flow led to very high tide levels along the coast today \nwith water reaching the dunes at many spots along the coast. With \nthat flow persisting through the middle of the week, and being in a \nseasonal high tide time, coastal flooding will continue to be a \nconcern on Gulf facing beaches daily. \n\nShould a tropical system develop, we could see increasing long \nperiod swells push into the coast to further increase tide levels, \nand bring an increased risk of rip currents.",
